{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Go up to this mountain of the Abarim</b> – The name of this mountain is \"Mount Nevo,\" as he explains in the portion of Ha'azinu (Deuteronomy 32:49) and in Zot HaBerakhah (ibid. 34:1). But they call it \"the mountain of the Abarim\" because it is at the crossings of the Jordan, and from there they will cross over to the land of Canaan, as it says here (in Ha'azinu and in Zot HaBerakhah) \"which faces Jericho,\" and there they crossed the Jordan, as it is stated: \"And the people came up from the Jordan and encamped at Gilgal, at the eastern edge of Jericho\" (Joshua 4:19). And it is not a commandment that the Holy One, blessed be He, should command us to do so now, for if so, he would be obligated to go up there immediately. Rather, its meaning is: you will go up to the mountain of the Abarim and see the land, for because He commanded him \"To these you shall divide the land\" (Numbers 26:53), He informed him that not through you will it be divided, for you will go up to the summit of the mountain of the Abarim before Israel travels from the land of Moav, and you will die there, and you will not attain of the land anything but the seeing. And likewise, \"Take for yourself Joshua\" (Numbers 27:18) — when your day comes, you shall take Joshua. And the verse completes the account (Numbers 27:22), telling that Moses did so with a whole heart, and this is the action that he mentions (Deuteronomy 31:7) at the time of Moses' passing, as they said: he recited the song, he and Hosea son of Nun:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:16.877709614Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}